phase P12
title Restore
description
    **Class:** not readily verifiable.

    **Kind:** verification-suite.

    **Goal:** slice 2, durability, second half: restore, key versions and record migration (T20, absorbing T11 and T26).

    **Verified by:**

    - the slice's suite half: parts and the Spec model's section green, killing table-cell mutants of the model,
      reviewed and audited by its own adversarial auditor (skill:policy-testing#audit-a-suite);
    - then the production half through the production adapter: model-predicted runs pass, with its coverage rows in
      @plan.Runtime's verification;
    - skill:check-task per Task; `nub run check` at zero.

    **One iteration:** a slice's suite half first (Spec change, model section, parts, model mutation, audit), then its
    production half built test-first against model-predicted runs, then the benchmark replay and the gates. When
    production and a case disagree, the Spec decides and the fix cites its rule.

    **Exit criteria:**

    - the slice's suite half is green on the model, mutant-proven by model mutation, reviewed and audited before its
      production half starts;
    - the slice's new codes are declared, enabled and covered by the combination gate before it closes.

    **False pass:** the production adapter doing work the runtime should; a case edited to pass.

    **Caught by:** mutants run against the production adapter, a review section on the adapter's role, and the audit.

    **Gates:**

    - falsify on both adapters (`packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/`, each oracle's mutants file);
    - `nub run check`, which rejects a planted lint violation (`tools/lint-bomb/`).

    **Failure limit:** 2.

    **Stops:**

    - a guarantee change or one that can't be met;
    - public entry-point names or a large public API change.

    **User's read:** each Spec revision, each changed expected result with its reason, the internal module layout, small
    public API calls.

    **Draws from:**

    - skill:policy-verification (prove a suite; the coverage record);
    - skill:policy-testing (audit a suite; test first: module integration tests through the public interface, no unit
      tests of internals, and the scenarios that enforce each Invariant written before the code);
    - skill:to-spec for the area's Spec and skill:to-design per module;
    - skill:policy-convergence at the threshold; skill:review at the cadence;
    - skill:playbook-build (long runs), skill:execute-task, skill:check-task; skill:diagnose when a case fails
      unexplained.
tasks
    T11
        title       Restore area complete
        status      done
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@task.V3.T10]
        description
            Cover @spec.Restore's guarantee map with oracles and cases.

            **Why now:** the suite has no Restore area, so the suite can't judge what a runtime restores.

            **Read first:** conformance/README.md (the Restore rows of the
            guarantee map, and C168 in its round-14 carry list), @spec.Restore, @spec.Conformance.R4. Blocked
            by @task.V3.T10, whose disk reader it reads.

            **Approach:** oracles `restore` (anchored to bytes the suite owns) and `quarantine`; cases, the
            migration wrong runtimes, ported cases, verdict compare and carry rows (C168, a crash between
            moving a lane and deleting the old name, record formats).

            - **May change:** the suite's restore area.
            - **Evidence:** this Task's comments.
            - **False pass:** restore judged from the runtime's own report → caught by anchoring to
              suite-owned bytes, and the audit.
            - **Gates:** falsify; `nub run check`.
            - **Stops:** a guarantee change.
            - **User's read:** Spec revisions, changed expected results.
        acceptance
            - Every Restore Invariant has its oracle or case;
            - each oracle kills its listed mutants;
            - verdicts match the old suite's or each difference is logged.
    T20
        title       Durability: restore, owed endings at boot, quarantine and forget (slice 2a)
        status      draft
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@task.V3.T19]
        satisfies   [@plan.Inspection.O2, @plan.Persistence.O4, @plan.Persistence.O5]
        description
            Build restore from @spec.Restore and @spec.Codec: reload, owed endings delivered at boot, record format 
            tags, unreadable-record quarantine and `forget` (slice 2a).

            **Why now:** write-ahead records exist but nothing reads them back, so a reload cannot restore actors or 
            deliver what was owed.

            **Read first:** @spec.Restore, @spec.Codec, @spec.WriteAhead, conformance/README.md (the `restore` row and 
            the carry list).

            **Split (orchestrator, 2026-11-12, wide consult W3):** key fingerprints, identity versions, saved-shape 
            resets, record migration and `previous` holds moved to @task.V3.T56, after @task.V3.T9: a saved shape is 
            defined only once @task.V3.T17 and @task.V3.T57 fix fields and fragments, and identity versions need T9's 
            value-named actors. Read code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/fable-wide-consult-2026-11-12.md (W3, 
            item 2).

            **Slice 2, durability (replan, accepted 2026-11-11), after @task.V3.T19:** absorbs @task.V3.T11's restore 
            half (the Restore area suite: oracles `restore` and `quarantine`, C168 and the record-format carry rows; 
            the migration wrong runtimes go to T56). Adds the Restore oracles, which re-judge every composite already 
            built, record format tags, and `forget` on the public route; the Restore and WriteAhead Specs gain the R2 
            `Store` Invariant and a host store section (@plan.Persistence.O1). Codes: sr rl fg. Read first: 
            code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/replan-2026-11-11.md (part 3, "2 Durability").

            **Brief** (proposed; source
            code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/core-task-briefs-2026-11-12.md): the sections below
            and the comments "Rules for every brief" and "Requirements (from core-task-briefs-2026-11-12.md)"
            are this Task's brief. The older comments are known gaps. Where this brief and the record disagree,
            the brief is newer.

            **Read first (added):** @spec.Restore (all); @spec.Codec (all); @spec.WriteAhead (R4, R5, R9);
            @spec.Actors (R10, R20, R21); conformance/README.md (the `restore` row
            and the carry list); `conformance/owes.ts` (the Restore and Codec rows).

            **Record differences:**

            - "Delivers owed endings at boot" in the record means at the first Start after a boot: a boot itself
              delivers and parks nothing (@spec.Restore.R1; @spec.Actors.R10).
            - `conformance/owes.ts` gives T20 Restore I12 and I14; they are @task.V3.T56's since the split
              (key versions, saved-shape resets). The builder moves those rows to T56; Restore I13 and I15 (lane
              quarantine) stay here.
            - Code `fg` is missing from `MODEL_CODES` (`conformance/parts/codes.ts`); step 1 adds it.

            **Decisions already made:**

            - T20 is restore only; key fingerprints, identity versions, saved-shape resets, migration steps and
              `previous` holds are T56's (orchestrator, 2026-11-12).
            - A boot sorts by record key and delivers and parks nothing; Start delivers (T54 built `start`).
              Record-key order is the order (@spec.Restore.R3).
            - Decided (orchestrator, 2026-11-12): no creation-order record keys are built.
            - Decided (orchestrator, 2026-11-12): an ending's `value` is encoded by the commit's declared Schema;
              with none, the value is dropped and the row keeps its tag only.
            - unknown { Restored } is derived at Start and stored nowhere.
            - One `format` number, 1, for all kinds. No older format exists, so no migration step is written; a
              newer, null or non-integer `format` is quarantined (@spec.Restore.R5).
            - "forget" is two routes: an identity's forget (@spec.Actors.R21: removes the actor and owed records,
              closes the rows, keeps a Held lane's record unbound) and `runtime.dispose({ forget: true })`
              (@spec.Restore.R10: deletes the scope).
            - Codes: sr rl fg.

            **Parts:** every part built by then, each script cut at every crash point and reloaded; S13 and S08
            under stress.

            **Requirements:** R-1 to R-14, in the comment "Requirements (from core-task-briefs-2026-11-12.md)";
            each is red first.

            **Steps:**

            1. **Suite half.** The Restore and Codec model sections; oracles `restore` and `quarantine`; code
               `fg` in `MODEL_CODES`; the parts' reload operations on the model. **Check:** `parts` and
               `model-mutation` green.
            2. **Open item first:** how a row's `value` is encoded; answered above (decided, orchestrator,
               2026-11-12).
            3. **Codec** (R-9, R-10): the canonical byte codec and its vectors; the three record Schemas.
            4. **Boot** (R-1, R-2, R-7, R-8, R-13): decode, sort by key, quarantine, install; wake the Owing
               identities.
            5. **Start from bytes** (R-3 to R-6): `start` already delivers; restore supplies the rows and lanes.
            6. **forget** (R-11, R-12), both routes.
            7. **Template items:** codes sr rl fg declared; the Restore oracles re-judge every composite; R-14;
               the combination gate; held-out seeds; the Fable sample; the benchmark replay.

            **Traps:**

            - **Restore read from memory** passes without a codec. R-1's fresh Runtime shares nothing with the
              writer but bytes.
            - **Boot must not deliver.** Delivery is Start's. `GEN-pay-search-25`'s red was the model's fault,
              fixed in `974ad3a1`: the model allows that restore at the boot or at the adoption. Production does
              it at Start only.
            - **Order across lanes:** all owed rows first, then each lane's derived endings.
            - **Restore's LaneRekeyed and OldVersionLane** name one thing twice, and both are T56's; if R-8
              reaches a re-keyed or old-version lane, stop and ask.
            - **`Owing` (Restore `model`) is not `Owed`** (one ledger row, WriteLanes `model`).
            - **Invariants left to cover from their Spec text** (rule above): Restore I9 and I19 have Spec tests (T14,
              T5) but no oracle or case yet; write one each.

            **Must not decide alone:** how a row's `value` is encoded when JSON cannot carry it (decided, above);
            creation-order keys (decided: none, above); what happens to a saved row whose operation is gone (ask;
            the lane rule is R-8, the row rule is unstated); any version or key-fingerprint tag (T56); the name
            RecordUndecodable; whether an identity's forget is allowed while its Attempt is on the wire.

            **Handoffs:**

            - **T56:** the record Schemas and where a version tag would sit; the quarantine path; Restore I12 and
              I14.
            - **T58:** the reload scripts the browser slice replays on IndexedDB in Chrome and Safari.
            - **T9:** boot's wake list by identity; the boot queue for a send to a persisted actor.

            - **May change:** `src/`.
            - **Evidence:** this Task's comments.
            - **False pass:** restore read from memory, not bytes → caught by the restore oracle.
            - **Gates:** the suite; falsify.
            - **Stops:** a guarantee change.
        acceptance
            The Codec area passes on production, and the Restore area does except the Invariants @task.V3.T56 owns (key 
            versions, saved-shape resets, `previous` holds).
            - Every Restore Invariant this Task owns has its oracle or case and each oracle kills its mutants (from 
              @task.V3.T11).
            - A reload restores actors from bytes, delivers owed endings at boot, quarantines an unreadable record, and 
              `forget` removes an identity's records, each passing cases on production.
            - The slice's generated runs pass on production, and its benchmark replay matches the work fingerprint.
            - Declared: sr rl fg are in the production and Spec-model capability lists, and the feature-matrix gate 
              shows none of them as owed.
            - Enabled: the parts built earlier declare those codes among their needs, the generator draws their 
              operations, and the app mix runs them.
            - Covered: the combination gate counts the pairs among implemented codes, fails an unexercised interacting 
              pair with no dated waiver, and passes before this Task closes.
            - Guards: production's first-contact reds are recorded and classified, held-out seeds pass, and a sample of 
              the model's expectations is read by a different model family (Fable).
        comment     2026-11-15T09:19:39Z
            Found while classifying Invariants (code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/test-system-2026-11-15.md, 
            section 3.6): Codec I7 says no lane record has a mayHaveApplied field; src/core/record.ts defines and 
            writes it. One of the two is wrong.

    T56
        title       Durability: release versions, key fingerprints and record migration (slice 2b)
        status      draft
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@task.V3.T21]
        satisfies   [@plan.Persistence.O5, @plan.Runtime.O3]
        description
            Build release versions on production: key fingerprints, identity versions, saved-shape resets with record 
            migration, and `previous` holds checked against Restore R6 (slice 2b).

            **Why now:** an upgraded release must reset or keep each saved record correctly. Split from @task.V3.T20 
            (orchestrator, 2026-11-12, wide consult W3) because a saved shape is defined only once @task.V3.T17 and 
            @task.V3.T57 fix private and computed fields and fragments, and identity versions need @task.V3.T9's 
            value-named actors; built before them it would be built on Pay-shaped machines and rebuilt.

            **Read first:** @spec.Restore, @spec.Codec, @task.V3.T20 (the restore it extends), 
            code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/fable-wide-consult-2026-11-12.md (W3, item 2).

            Absorbs @task.V3.T26 (record migration, @plan.Persistence.O5; real storage shapes are IndexedDB structured 
            clone from @task.V3.T58) and @task.V3.T11's migration wrong runtimes. Codes: up pg. Part built: S25 [A]. 
            Bench: the S25 upgrade boot. Blocked by @task.V3.T21 for the slice order (W3, item 4).

            **Phase-out, step 5 (the user accepted 2026-11-11):** the Stamp twin (release 1 and 2 keys; `up`) is 
            written in `fixtures/`; run the hand cases on it on production; write production mutants per oracle the 
            slice judges, mapped by Invariant from the round-14 mutants; delete `stamp.ts` once the derived reference 
            equals it. Read code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/conformance-phase-out-2026-11-11.md.

            - **May change:** `src/`, the conformance fixtures, model and generator.
            - **Evidence:** this Task's comments.

            False pass: a reset judged from the runtime's report, not the bytes → caught by the restore oracle and the 
            Stamp twin's round-14 verdicts.
            Gates: the suite; falsify; the matrix and combination gates; `nub run check`.
            Stops: a guarantee change.
        acceptance
            The Restore area passes on production in full, with every Restore Invariant's oracle or case killing its 
            mutants.
            - Record migration passes cases on production: a changed saved shape resets its definition's records with 
              the writes in flight kept (from @task.V3.T26, @plan.Persistence.O5).
            - Key fingerprints, identity versions and `previous: [{ key }]` holds pass cases on production (Restore R6).
            - The slice's generated runs pass on production, and its benchmark replay matches the work fingerprint.
            - Declared: up pg are in the production and Spec-model capability lists, and the feature-matrix gate shows 
              none of them as owed.
            - Enabled: S25 [A] and the parts built earlier declare those codes among their needs, the generator draws 
              their operations, and the app mix runs them.
            - Covered: the combination gate counts the pairs among implemented codes, fails an unexercised interacting 
              pair with no dated waiver, and passes before this Task closes.
            - Guards: production's first-contact reds are recorded and classified, held-out seeds pass, and a sample of 
              the model's expectations is read by a different model family (Fable).
            - Phase-out step 5: the Stamp twin exists in `fixtures/` and `stamp.ts` is deleted; the same verdict per 
              case on round-14 and production.
            - Phase-out checks (the four validations): no lost catch in the catch matrix; the same coverage and 
              feature-matrix rows; the derived reference equal to the hand rows wherever both exist; Compile static 
              cases green on each fixture's `Compiled`.
